          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='agilitybandits-logotype'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Agility Bandits sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='agilitybandits'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Agility Bandits began as an identity for sharing dog-agility content and later developed
        into a complete blog brand. I wanted the design to keep the personality of Roi, my
        red-and-white Border Collie, while also expressing the speed, humour, and unpredictability
        of the sport. The identity needed to work across profile images, navigation, page graphics,
        and larger website layouts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The logo combined several strong elements: a detailed dog portrait, a bandit theme, and
            a bold display wordmark. The main challenge was bringing them together without making
            the identity difficult to recognise when it appeared at a smaller size.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Capture Roi's recognisable colouring and expression</li>
            <li>
              Make the bandit idea clear without letting it overpower the dog
            </li>
            <li>
              Create a visual style that could extend beyond the primary logo
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/logo-agilitybandits-600.webp'
            data-src='logo-agilitybandits-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='473'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Agility Bandits logo with a red-and-white Border Collie wearing a bandit mask above a Western-style wordmark'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Character-led primary logo featuring Roi and the bandit wordmark
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I simplified Roi's fur into clear vector shapes while keeping his natural colours and
              the details that make him recognisable.
            </li>
            <li>
              The eye mask and Western-inspired lettering connect the dog portrait with the
              “Bandits” name without introducing another separate symbol.
            </li>
            <li>
              I created full, horizontal, and icon-focused versions so the identity could adapt to
              social media, web layouts, and printed material.
            </li>
            <li>
              Greens and dark neutrals form the supporting palette, allowing Roi's red-and-white
              colouring to provide the main contrast.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-agilitybandits-elements-600.webp'
            data-src='design-agilitybandits-elements-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='441'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Agility Bandits vector graphics showing speed lines and falling dog agility obstacles'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Motion-inspired obstacle graphics communicate speed and chaotic fun
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block'>
          <h2>From logo to visual system</h2>
          <p>
            The identity needed more than a logo to support a complete website. I created speed
            lines and tipping agility obstacles that could add energy to a layout without repeating
            the main character everywhere. Using the same angles, line weight, and green accents
            keeps the different elements connected.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Used directional lines to suggest speed and acceleration</li>
            <li>
              Turned familiar agility equipment into playful decorative graphics
            </li>
            <li>
              Applied the elements across the header, footer, and page layouts
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/graphics-agilitybandits-elements-600.webp'
            data-src='graphics-agilitybandits-elements-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='453'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Agility Bandits logo and obstacle graphics applied to the website header and footer'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Logo and supporting graphics applied across the website
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The finished system gives Agility Bandits a recognisable personality that works from a small
        social icon to a complete website layout. Roi makes the brand personal, while the supporting
        graphics provide enough variation to use the identity without placing the full logo
        everywhere. This project helped me understand how one central character can become the
        starting point for a wider and more flexible visual system.
      </p>
    </div>

